The attached image visually depicts the dendrimer-like alpha-D-glucan nanoparticles (Nano-11) as described in the PDF. It illustrates their spherical shape and characteristic highly branched internal structure, emanating from a central core and becoming increasingly dense towards the surface. This dendrimer-like morphology, a result of the phytoglycogen's inherent branching due to the lack of a debranching enzyme, is crucial for Nano-11's high molecular density, ability to electrostatically adsorb antigens, and its role in enhancing antigen uptake by dendritic cells and stimulating an immune response.